{"name":"Galley Gold","hex":"#d5aa5e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"galley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, slender ship propelled primarily by oars, whether having masts and sails or not; usually a rowed warship used in the Mediterranean from the 16th century until the modern era.","A light, open boat used on the Thames by customhouse officers, press gangs, and also for pleasure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/galley"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Galley","blocks":[{"text":"A galley is a type of ship optimised for propulsion by oars. Galleys were historically used for warfare, trade, and piracy mostly in the seas surrounding Europe. It developed in the Mediterranean world during antiquity and continued to exist in various forms until the early 19th century. It typically had a long, slender hull, shallow draft, and often a low freeboard. Most types of galleys also had sails that could be used in favourable winds, but they relied primarily on oars to move independently of winds and currents or in battle. The term \"galley\" originates from the Ancient Greek word \"galea\" which was used to describe a narrow vessel with resemblance to the shape of a dogfish. It is believed the English form of the term came into use around 1300. It has occasionally been used for unrelated vessels with similar military functions as the galley but which were not Mediterranean in origin, such as medieval Scandinavian longships, 16th-century Acehnese ghalis and 18th-century North American gunboats."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sbonski_de_Passabon-Galere_a_la_voille.jpeg/330px-Sbonski_de_Passabon-Galere_a_la_voille.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galley","wikidata":"Q188924"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Valcour_canadianarchive_c013202k.jpg/500px-Valcour_canadianarchive_c013202k.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Valcour_canadianarchive_c013202k.jpg/500px-Valcour_canadianarchive_c013202k.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"United States ships at the Battle of Valcour Island depicting several \"row galleys\" similar in function but based on very different designs from Mediterranean galleys; watercolour by Charles Randle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AValcour_canadianarchive_c013202k.jpg"},